  "rationale": "From the fleet facts, pipelined_m is clearly the winner among my assigned families: it has 42 feasible designs, best area 796, and 4 designs on the merged Pareto front. The plain pipelined family has 40 feasible but best area 1645 and 0 front designs, so it is dominated and I drop it entirely. I focus all 42 evaluations on pipelined_m, narrowing the ranges to the region that already produced front designs: data_width 12-16 (the accuracy constraint max_abs_err <= 2^-10 = 0.000976562 requires roughly 10+ bits of accuracy; with n_iter 10-14, data_width 12-16 should suffice while keeping area minimal), n_iter 10-14 (enough micro-rotations for the accuracy target without excessive area), angle_guard -2..0 (negative guard reduces angle-path width and atan-LUT size, saving area; the sin/cos accuracy is governed by data_width and n_iter, not angle_guard), frac_guard 0..1 (minimal extra fractional bits to stay within the accuracy budget), m 2..4 (m=2-4 balances register count against Fmax to meet the >=1 MSPS throughput constraint; m=2 gives the best Fmax headroom). The throughput constraint (>=1 MSPS) is the binding one for pipelined_m: with m=2 and N=10-14, latency is ceil(N/m)+2 = 7-9 cycles, and Fmax needs to be >=100 MHz which is very achievable on Artix-7 for this datapath width. The area objective (min luts_plus_ffs, HV ref 1500) means I should push data_width and n_iter toward the lower end of the feasible range, and angle_guard/frac_guard toward the minimum. The accuracy_bits objective (max, HV ref 10) rewards slightly higher n_iter or data_width, so the Pareto front will span the trade-off between area and accuracy within these ranges."
